	('09192ffc-a0cd-4455-a88f-136aad4f89f6', 'Learning on Random Balls is Sufficient for Estimating (Some) Graph Parameters', 'Theoretical analyses for graph learning methods often assume a complete observation of the input graph. Such an assumption might not be useful for handling any-size graphs due to the scalability issues in practice. In this work, we develop a theoretical framework for graph classification problems in the partial observation setting (i.e., subgraph samplings). Equipped with insights from graph limit theory, we propose a new graph classification model that works on a randomly sampled subgraph and a novel topology to characterize the representability of the model. Our theoretical framework contributes a theoretical validation of mini-batch learning on graphs and leads to new learning-theoretic results on generalization bounds as well as size-generalizability without assumptions on the input.', '{"Maehara, Takanori","NT, Hoang"}', '3b78fba9-1e0e-4622-8bcd-018ea1b8d903', 'submitted', 'https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2021/file/08f36fcf88c0a84c19a6ed437b9cbcc9-Paper.pdf', NULL, '2025-02-14 14:40:03.219852+00', '[-0.012395153,-0.028777866,0.008815427,-0.05251237,0.031775825,0.03241297,0.012127545,0.04379767,0.026678495,0.017233986,0.031876404,-0.04856749,0.04767453,0.07433206,-0.02631192,0.021635978,-0.018797446,0.050931893,-0.025702992,-0.02605173,0.0038797068,-0.025386894,-0.01964699,-0.07544018,0.060004868,0.0050830846,-0.00465788,-0.06948041,-0.045106094,-0.26063064,0.02464667,-0.022922311,0.07351651,-0.048470728,0.03071419,-0.0574954,0.009999765,0.027960418,-0.03577862,0.07652204,0.0026387563,0.0041078576,-0.047025573,-0.012355613,-0.042054527,-0.05125309,0.008164473,-0.016084919,-0.043532442,-0.056102023,0.0126341125,-0.045013834,0.046608835,0.015644688,0.018132158,0.053076252,0.017271055,0.014582719,0.054733805,0.047415495,0.02246504,0.04823788,-0.12261599,0.024878614,0.014972883,0.06458088,-0.08327254,0.008721492,0.026071187,0.0